From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:46:18 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 35/61] hjviewer: wake-on-LAN: a bound PC tells how to wake it
 and stays up after a resume

The service of a bound PC tells hjv-api, in its sync, what a relay on the PC's
network needs to wake it (design v3 8-2), and a PC woken with nobody at it
stays up long enough for its owner to connect. The code is in hjviewer_wol.rs
and hjviewer_account/pc.rs; beside them:

- lib.rs: mod hjviewer_wol, desktop only.
- Cargo.toml: the windows crate's Win32_System_Power (GetPwrCapabilities,
  GetSystemPowerStatus, DevicePowerEnumDevices, SetThreadExecutionState).
- hjviewer_e2e.rs: --hjv-e2e-wol-report <mac> <ipv4/prefix> <gateway>, one
  sync of the PC's binding carrying the report of one wired card with these
  values, signed and sent as the sync task's are, for the WOL E2E (design v3
  8-10); it exits 1 unless hjv-api answered 200.

The report has the PC's wired physical cards: those default-net sees with a
MAC and an IPv4 address whose network class key, the one with the adapter's
GUID as NetCfgInstanceId (a card no longer there leaves a key with the same
description), is NCF_PHYSICAL and 802.3 (*PhysicalMediaType 14). That leaves
out the kernel debug, Hyper-V, Bluetooth PAN, VPN and WAN miniport adapters
and Wi-Fi; a USB card stays, marked bus "usb". A card gives its MAC, IPv4
addresses, gateway and the gateway's MAC, description, bus, link speed,
driver provider, version and date, the wake keywords as the registry has
them, and whether Windows lists it among the devices armed to wake the PC
(the list powercfg /devicequery wake_armed shows); the PC adds S3, S4, S5,
the hibernation file and modern standby (GetPwrCapabilities), fast startup
(HiberbootEnabled, or a policy that requires it), mains power and its uptime.
Facts only; hjv-api judges them, and no app gets the MAC. A report is held
to four cards, four addresses a card and 64 characters a value, far below
the sync's 16 KiB for the values registries have.

The sync (hjviewer_account/pc.rs): beside the sync task a collector looks
every minute and, while the PC is bound, collects in spawn_blocking once the
last report is ten minutes old, so first a minute after the service starts
or the PC is bound; an unbound PC collects and sends nothing. Every sync of
the binding carries wol_hash, the SHA-256 of the report's JSON without the
uptime (empty while there is no report), and the whole report (wol) until a
sync of that binding carrying it got a 200, and once more after an answer
with send_wol: true. A bind request and the report after the binding went
carry neither. hjv-api refuses a sync above 16 KiB as a whole, the trust list
with it, so a report that would not fit (registry values of control
characters or quotes, which are escaped twice on the way) stays on the PC and
its sync goes with the summary alone.

After a resume (the sync task's clock jump) a bound PC asks Windows to stay
up, SetThreadExecutionState(ES_CONTINUOUS | ES_SYSTEM_REQUIRED), for ten
minutes, from a thread of its own as Windows ties the request to the thread;
another resume meanwhile extends it. Sleep someone asks for (the power
button, the lid, the Start menu) still goes ahead.

A build without HJV_SERVER, HJV_LOGIN_SEAL_PUBKEY or HJV_TRUST_PUBKEYS starts
no sync task, so it neither collects nor holds, as 1.5.1. Without a report a
sync's payload is what it was, the shared vectors' (wol_hash empty, no wol).
Desktop platforms other than Windows have no report and no hold.

For hjv-api: wol_hash is to be compared for equality only, and an empty one
(the PC's first minute, a platform without a report, the class keys
unreadable) leaves the stored report as it is. A summary that keeps coming
without its report, whatever send_wol asks, is of a report held back for its
size.

Tests: on a synthesized registry and adapter list shaped like a real PC's (the
wired card after the key of a card no longer there with its description, the
kernel debug adapter, a WAN miniport, USB Wi-Fi, Bluetooth PAN, a VPN tunnel,
Hyper-V and the loopback; MACs of 00:00:5e:00:53:00/24, RFC 5737 addresses and
made-up GUIDs) the report is design v3 8-2's example; a USB card stays marked
and what Windows did not say is null; the summary leaves the uptime out and
follows a wake setting; the report goes whole once per binding and summary
and again when asked; a resume during the hold extends it; the E2E command's
report and its arguments; and the sync task against an in-memory hjv-api on a
paused clock: an unbound PC sends nothing and its bind request no report, a
sync within 70 seconds of the binding the whole report and the next its
summary, the report again after send_wol, nothing of it in the report after
the binding went, and a report too large for a sync stays on the PC while the
syncs go on with its summary.

Known limits: the hold is not yet tried on a PC that woke by a magic packet
(the field test with the Windows build, design v3 7-6 and 8-7), nor on a
modern standby PC. A network that changes under a running PC shows in the
report within ten minutes.

(Design v3, work package W3: patch 0035.)

